        "starterCode": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class TwoPointers {\n    public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {\n        int left = 0;\n        int right = s.length() - 1;\n\n        while (left < right) {\n            while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(left))) left++;\n            while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(right))) right--;\n            if (Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(left)) != Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(right))) {\n                return false;\n            }\n            left++;\n            right--;\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(isPalindrome(\"A man, a plan, a canal: Panama\"));\n        System.out.println(isPalindrome(\"race a car\"));\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>⚔️ The Two-Pointer Collision & Collision Convergence</h2>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>💡 The Teacher's Perspective:</strong><br />\n  Nested <code>for</code> loops are the #1 reason algorithms fail interview time limits, burning O(N²) quadratic time. The <strong>Two-Pointer technique</strong> is one of the most elegant weapons in computer science: by moving two pointers simultaneously, you solve search problems in a single, blazing O(N) linear sweep!\n</div>\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--analogy\">\n  <strong>🌍 Walking Across a Bridge:</strong><br />\n  Imagine two friends standing at opposite ends of a long wooden footbridge walking toward each other. Instead of one person walking the whole length over and over, both converge in the middle, inspecting all pairs in half the time!\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>🎯 What We Are Learning & Why:</strong> <em>Welcome to the proving grounds of linear coordinate combat. When arrays are sorted or bounded, nested loops waste quadratic cycles. Converging pointers slice across boundaries in strictly linear O(N) time.</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>The Two-Pointer technique is one of the most revered weapons in competitive algorithms. Instead of fixing one index and scanning through all remaining elements with a nested loop (which burns O(N^2) time), we initialize two distinct pointers that traverse the sequence simultaneously.</p>\n  <p>In opposite-direction convergence (Two Sum II, Container With Most Water), one pointer begins at index <code>0</code> and the other at index <code>N - 1</code>. Depending on the condition, we advance <code>left++</code> or retreat <code>right--</code>, discarding huge swaths of invalid candidate pairs in single mathematical steps.</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Blueprint</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\nArray: [ 2,  7, 11, 15 ], Target = 18\nStep 1: left -> [2], right -> [15]  Sum = 17 < 18 (Need larger sum -> left++)\nStep 2: left -> [7], right -> [15]  Sum = 22 > 18 (Need smaller sum -> right--)\nStep 3: left -> [7], right -> [11]  Sum = 18 == Target! FOUND in O(N)!\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Algorithmic Invariants & Best Practices</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Directional Invariant:</strong> Always verify if the collection is sorted or monotonic before deploying converging pointers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Duplicate Skipping:</strong> In problems like 3Sum, remember to skip identical consecutive numbers to avoid duplicate triplets.</li>\n  <li><strong>Equi-directional Pointers:</strong> Used for fast/slow cycles (Floyd) and array partitions (partitioning around a pivot in QuickSort).</li>\n</ul>"
