        "title": "Difference Arrays & Coordinate Compression: Batch Interval Updates",
        "language": "java",
        "starterCode": "public class DifferenceArray {\n    private int[] diff;\n\n    public DifferenceArray(int[] nums) {\n        diff = new int[nums.length];\n        diff[0] = nums[0];\n        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {\n            diff[i] = nums[i] - nums[i - 1];\n        }\n    }\n\n    // O(1) interval update: add val to [i, j]\n    public void update(int i, int j, int val) {\n        diff[i] += val;\n        if (j + 1 < diff.length) {\n            diff[j + 1] -= val;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // O(N) reconstruct the final array\n    public int[] result() {\n        int[] res = new int[diff.length];\n        res[0] = diff[0];\n        for (int i = 1; i < diff.length; i++) {\n            res[i] = res[i - 1] + diff[i];\n        }\n        return res;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] initial = {0, 0, 0, 0, 0};\n        DifferenceArray da = new DifferenceArray(initial);\n        da.update(1, 3, 5); // Add 5 to index 1..3\n        da.update(2, 4, 3); // Add 3 to index 2..4\n        int[] finalArr = da.result();\n        for (int val : finalArr) {\n            System.out.print(val + \" \"); // Output: 0 5 8 8 3\n        }\n    }\n}",
        "content": "<h2>⚔️ Difference Arrays & Coordinate Compression: Batch Interval Updates</h2>\n\n<div class=\"teacher-callout teacher-callout--intuition\">\n  <strong>🎯 What We Are Learning & Why:</strong> <em>When hundreds of thousands of operations modify intervals of the battlefield simultaneously, naive loops trigger catastrophic O(Q × N) latency. Difference Arrays execute range additions in O(1) time!</em>\n</div>\n\n<p>A <strong>Difference Array</strong> <code>diff</code> is the inverse companion of the Prefix Sum array. It is defined such that <code>diff[i] = nums[i] - nums[i - 1]</code>.</p>\n  <p>Its mathematical superpower is converting an entire range increment of value <em>V</em> over interval $[L, R]$ into exactly two single-point modifications: <code>diff[L] += V</code> and <code>diff[R + 1] -= V</code>. After applying <em>Q</em> interval updates in O(Q) time, a single prefix sum sweep reconstructs the entire mutated array in O(N)!</p>\n\n<h3>Visual Topology & Memory Blueprint</h3>\n<pre class=\"ascii-diagram\">\nOriginal Array: [ 0,  0,  0,  0,  0 ]\nBatch operation 1: Add 10 to [1, 3] -> diff[1] += 10, diff[4] -= 10\nBatch operation 2: Add 5 to [2, 4]  -> diff[2] += 5,  diff[5] (out of bounds)\ndiff array:    [ 0, 10,  5,  0, -10 ]\nRunning Prefix Sum:\nres:           [ 0, 10, 15, 15,   5 ] <-- Correctly reflects both overlapping operations!\n</pre>\n\n<h3>Algorithmic Invariants & Best Practices</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Boundary Guard:</strong> Always ensure R + 1 < length before subtracting the value to prevent index out of bounds.</li>\n  <li><strong>Coordinate Compression:</strong> When intervals span huge domains (e.g. 1 to 10^9) with few updates, sort unique coordinates and map to 0..2K.</li>\n  <li><strong>Classic Trials:</strong> Corporate Flight Bookings (LeetCode 1109), Range Addition (LeetCode 370), Meeting Rooms II.</li>\n</ul>"
